<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Jani Beg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1357">1357</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Also known as Jani Beg Khan, Janibeg, Djanibek · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/borjigin">Borjigin</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Jani Beg was khan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a> from 1342 to 1357, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/uzbek-khan">Uzbek Khan</a>. During his siege of the Genoese port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feodosia">Caffa</a> in 1346 plague broke out among his army, and ships from Caffa may have carried the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-death">Black Death</a> towards <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. In 1357 he invaded <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a> and executed the Chupanid ruler <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malik-ashraf">Malik Ashraf</a>, but he died soon after, and the Horde fell into two decades of civil war.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/jani-beg.jpg" alt="Jani Beg" width="320" height="403" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: 1405-1409 · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Jani_Beg_Khan_enthroned,_in_Timurid_genealogy_1405-1409_(Topkapi_Sarayi_M%C3%BCzesi,_H2152)_f.38b.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>After his father Öz Beg died in 1341, Jani Beg's older brother and rival Tini Beg was in the east, possibly preparing an attack. As soon as Tini Beg entered the central lands he was killed by Jani Beg's followers, and a younger brother, Khiḍr Beg, was also killed on Jani Beg's orders. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kurultai">kurultai</a> elected Jani Beg khan in 1342 with the support of his mother Taydula Khatun. He kept his father's policy of centralisation and integrated the old territories of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/orda-khan">Orda Khan</a> back into the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/golden-horde">Golden Horde</a>, though the Blue Horde never submitted, especially after a major revolt in 1342.</p><p>Like his father he supported the house of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> and its heads, Simeon and Ivan II, and Russian chroniclers called him the good tsar. He interfered in Moscow's relations with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suzdal">Suzdal</a>, backed anti-Muscovite elements in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ryazan">Ryazan</a> and contributed to Tver's fragmentation. In 1342 he briefly taxed the Russian Church, restoring its exemption in September 1347 and confirming it in 1351. When the throne of Vladimir was contested after Simeon's death in 1353, he sided with Ivan II, keeping the balance among the principalities.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>In September 1343 a Venetian noble, Andreolo Civran, killed a Mongol official in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/don-river">Tanais</a>, and Jani Beg expelled all the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latins</a> from the Italian colonies. He demanded that Civran be judged by a Mongol court; the Genoese and Venetians formed a temporary alliance and imposed a trade embargo, and Jani Beg sent more troops to the Genoese colony of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feodosia">Kaffa</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crimea">Crimea</a>. The siege there lasted three years, with high losses on both sides, while boats carried supplies through the harbour.</p><p>According to an Italian source, Gabriel de Mussis of Piacenza, writing in 1348, plague broke out among Jani Beg's troops and they catapulted infected corpses into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/feodosia">Kaffa</a>. This story is disputed, and it is thought more likely that rats with infected fleas passed from his camp to the city. Genoese sailors then sailed from Kaffa to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genoa">Genoa</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/messina">Messina</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>. In the winter of 1356–1357 he attacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>, took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tabriz">Tabriz</a> and installed his own governor, with the support of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shirvanshah">Shirvanshah</a>; coins were minted in the city. The governor soon declared independence, and the region was annexed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jalayirid-sultanate">Jalayirids</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>Jani Beg fell ill and died in 1357 on his way back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sarai">Sarai</a>. Persian sources blame <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/berdibeg">Berdi Beg</a> or the beys, while the later Nikon Chronicle says the beys strangled him; the cause is unknown for lack of direct sources. Berdi Beg reigned after him and eliminated his brothers. His quarrel with the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latins</a> harmed the Horde's trade and caused a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/silver">silver</a> shortage and a decline in minting. He appears as Jambech, lord of Sarra, in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/catalan-atlas">Catalan Atlas</a> of 1375.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Jani Beg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1357 Khan <b>Janibeg</b> was murdered by his son Berdibeg, who was himself killed in 1359; the line of Batu died out, and two decades of civil war, the 'Great Troubles', followed, in which more than twenty khans held the throne in quick succession while the emir Mamai…</blockquote><a href="/empire/golden-horde">Golden Horde · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Its khans, including Berke, Uzbek Khan and <b>Jani Beg</b>, held authority over the whole Jochid realm.</blockquote><a href="/state/blue-horde">Blue Horde</a></li><li><blockquote>In 1357 <b>Jani Beg</b>, khan of the Golden Horde, invaded Azerbaijan at the invitation of exiles, captured Malik Ashraf and executed him, ending the dynasty.</blockquote><a href="/state/chupanids">Chupanids</a></li><li><blockquote>Relations with the Golden Horde were tense: during a siege by <b>Jani Beg</b> in 1346 plague broke out, and ships from Caffa may have helped carry it to Europe.</blockquote><a href="/state/genoese-gazaria">Genoese Gazaria</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in the Golden Horde. Died in Sarai. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p></section>
